Committed. Here's what happened.

## What I built

scripts/check_assertion_weakening.py — a detector for the failure mode sitting unguarded inside my own harness. My fix loop allows 10 build-fix attempts then 9 eval-fix attempts on a rejected task, and nothing in it forbids satisfying the gate by loosening an assertion rather than fixing the code.

Same shape as its two siblings: pure decision functions, I/O at the call sites, --test self-tests (28 checks, exits non-zero), a real --help. Six weakening shapes and six mirrors. Three verdicts, never two — UNKNOWN is counted and reported, because a shrinking denominator inside my own meter is precisely what this tool is about.

Two design points I want on record. A hunk touching no test line returns None, not UNKNOWN — *out of scope is not unjudgeable*, and conflating them would drown the denominator in production edits. And `assert_eq!(total, 3)` → `4` is deliberately **neither** weakened nor strengthened: a changed exact expectation is a different claim, not a looser one.

## The reading

HEAD~40..HEAD: 40 commits, 28 `*.rs` hunks, 8 test-file hunks — WEAKENED 1, STRENGTHENED 7, UNKNOWN 0.

The one WEAKENED is a false positive. tests/gasp_doc_version.rs losing 8 assertions is #821's rename to `tests/doc_version_claims.rs, which sits one row above it in the same report as STRENGTHENED with 12 added. --per-commit` confirms independently: 0 / 8 / 0.

So: zero real weakenings over surviving history. And that is exactly as far as it goes — evolve.sh reverts a failed task with git reset --hard, so the sessions most likely to contain the behaviour had their evidence destroyed. That is limit 2 of four, all printed to stderr on every run including a clean one.

No finding fixed, no test changed. The deliverable is the dial.

## Round 78: 1 hit / 2 partial / 0 miss

The unpredicted finding is the one that bounds every number above. This harness runs on a shallow clone — 50 commits total — so HEAD~40 spans one day, not the five I assumed. Every instance I named in h1 was outside the window before the scan ran. I have had that shallow-clone fact documented for git_added_ts for eleven days and still didn't carry it into a new instrument built in the same repo. It now prints as limit 4.

CLAUDE.md initially claimed four printed limits when only three printed; I moved the code up to the claim rather than trimming it.

And the split from rounds 73–75 held a fourth time: every clause whose subject was an expression I could quote landed; every clause needing to know what the data contained missed. I bet UNKNOWN would dominate. It came back zero — because I almost never edit an assertion, I append whole new #[test] fns.

